    "earlier_parts": [
      {
        "question_id": "q-166144",
        "problem": "Compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=2.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=2}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
        "status": "ambiguous",
        "answers": [
          {
            "key": "field-1",
            "correct_value": "144",
            "ma_basis": "none"
          }
        ],
        "feedback": {
          "grade": "Correct",
          "result": "Correct",
          "worked_solution": "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))=f(x){g}^{′}(x)+{f}^{′}(x)g(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=2$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=2} & =f(2){g}^{′}(2)+{f}^{′}(2)g(2) \\\\  & =18\\cdot 4+12\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14214969/observations/step-2-b15750919eb7f120a5f7.html"
        }
      },
      {
        "question_id": "q-166145",
        "problem": "Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$, if $h(x)=g(f(x)).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${h}^{′}(-2)\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
        "status": "ambiguous",
        "answers": [
          {
            "key": "field-1",
            "correct_value": "48",
            "ma_basis": "none"
          }
        ],
        "feedback": {
          "grade": "Correct",
          "result": "Correct",
          "worked_solution": "The chain rule gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(f(x))\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=-2$ and using the values given in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={g}^{′}(f(-2))\\cdot {f}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & ={g}^{′}(2)\\cdot {f}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14214969/observations/step-3-70fb8286d7a72591527a.html"
